                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="insights" class="ls-section" aria-labelledby="insights-heading">
            <div class="ls-section__intro">
                <p class="ls-kicker">08 · What it shows</p>
                <h2 id="insights-heading" class="ls-h2">Ten things the spectrum shows</h2>
            </div>

            <ol class="ls-insights">
                <li>
                    <h3>There is no best language, only a frontier</h3>
                    <p>
                        Put any two facets against each other and the languages form a curve. Speed costs
                        approachability, safety costs freedom, and expressive power costs readability for the next
                        person. What has changed is where the curve sits. Languages designed between 2009 and 2016,
                        such as Go, Kotlin, Swift and Rust, learned from their predecessors and got more of both
                        sides. Older languages are moving the same way through constant revision: today’s Java and
                        C# look little like their 2010 selves.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="map:fast-friendly">Show the trade-off</button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>The managed middle runs business software</h3>
                    <p>
                        Java, C#, Go and Kotlin share a profile: statically typed, garbage collected, fast enough,
                        with mature tools and long compatibility records. None of them tops a single facet, but they
                        score high on nearly all of them, and that is why most enterprise software lives here.
                        Boring is a feature when a system has to outlive its authors. C# was TIOBE’s language of the
                        year for 2025, and Java and C# both make the top six of IEEE Spectrum’s jobs ranking.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="compare:java csharp go">Compare Java, C# and Go</button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Types are winning, even in dynamic languages</h3>
                    <p>
                        In August 2025 TypeScript became the most used language on GitHub by monthly contributors,
                        up 66% in a year to 2.6 million, and it tops JetBrains’ Language Promise Index. The dynamic
                        languages are moving the same way. Python has type hints and fast checkers, PHP enforces
                        type declarations, Ruby has RBS and Sorbet, and Elixir 1.20 now type-checks every program
                        without annotations. No major language has gone the other way. AI is accelerating the shift:
                        GitHub credits typed languages for making AI-assisted code more reliable, and a 2025 study
                        found that 94% of compilation errors in LLM-generated TypeScript were type errors. The type
                        checker is what catches the model’s mistakes.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="rank:typescript">Show TypeScript’s rankings</button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Memory safety moved from preference to policy</h3>
                    <p>
                        Microsoft and Google’s Chromium team both found that around 70% of their serious security
                        vulnerabilities come from memory-safety bugs. In 2024 the White House urged software makers
                        to adopt memory-safe languages, and CISA and the FBI asked them to publish memory-safety
                        roadmaps by the end of 2025. The data backs the policy: as Android shifted new code to Rust,
                        memory-safety bugs fell from 76% of its vulnerabilities in 2019 to below 20% in 2025. C and
                        C++ aren’t going anywhere, and C++26 adds a hardened standard library, but new C or C++ in
                        security-sensitive code now needs a reason.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="map:safe-fast">Show safety against speed</button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Runtimes matter more than syntax</h3>
                    <p>
                        The JVM hosts Java, Kotlin, Scala and Clojure. .NET hosts C# and F#. The BEAM hosts Elixir and
                        Gleam. JavaScript engines run JavaScript, TypeScript, and code compiled from Kotlin, Dart, F#,
                        Gleam and ClojureScript. A language on an established runtime inherits its libraries, garbage
                        collector, concurrency model and deployment story on day one. That is how Kotlin went from
                        announcement to Android’s preferred language in eight years, and why Gleam was usable in
                        production at 1.0: it could call decades of Erlang libraries. Choose the runtime first
                        and the language second.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="lineage:kotlin">Trace Kotlin’s family</button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Admiration is a selection effect</h3>
                    <p>
                        The most admired languages are small: Odin at 82%, Elixir at 62% and Zig at 58% of users wanting
                        to keep using them, against 39% for Java and 42% for JavaScript. Part of that is who answers. People
                        who write Elixir went looking for it, while many people write Java because their employer
                        does. Bjarne Stroustrup put it best: there are only two kinds of languages, the ones people
                        complain about and the ones nobody uses. The impressive results are the exceptions:
                        TypeScript and Python stay well liked at scale, and Rust is admired by 67% of its users even
                        with 18% of developers using it.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="map:used-admired">Show use against admiration</button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Popularity depends on who you ask</h3>
                    <p>
                        TIOBE has C at number two and TypeScript at 38; GitHub has TypeScript first and C outside
                        its top ten. JetBrains’ survey puts Kotlin at 18% usage, which says as much about JetBrains’
                        audience as about Kotlin. Every index measures a population, not the world, which is why the
                        consensus here averages seven of them. The rankers are also warning that AI is eroding their
                        inputs: fewer searches and Stack Overflow questions as developers ask assistants instead.
                        Expect the next few years of rankings to be noisier.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="rank:c">Show C’s rankings</button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Concurrency is the new dividing line</h3>
                    <p>
                        Two decades ago the big argument was objects against functions. Now it is how a language
                        handles ten thousand things at once. Languages designed for multicore machines, such as Go,
                        Rust, Elixir and Gleam, have it built in. Older ones are retrofitting at great effort: Java’s
                        virtual threads, Python’s free-threaded build, Ruby’s Ractors, Swift 6’s data-race checks.
                        async/await, which C# brought to the mainstream in 2012, has since spread to JavaScript,
                        Python, Rust, Swift and Dart, bringing the “function colouring” problem with it.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="lens:concurrency">Show the concurrency lens</button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>AI makes the rich richer, and rewards strictness</h3>
                    <p>
                        Language models write better code in popular languages, because that is what they were
                        trained on. Research on multilingual code benchmarks found performance correlates with
                        popularity, and that models struggle with low-resource languages such as Julia, Lua, OCaml and
                        R. That is a headwind for every new language, which already faces a cold start. But AI also
                        rewards languages whose compilers catch mistakes: types, exhaustive matching, borrow checking.
                        My bet is that the languages that gain most from AI are the ones that are both popular and
                        strict: TypeScript, C#, Kotlin, Go and Rust.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="map:jobs-momentum">Show jobs against momentum</button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Learn a portfolio, not a favourite</h3>
                    <p>
                        Ideas move between families, as the family tree shows: pattern matching from ML to Rust, Swift
                        and C#, async from F# to C# to everyone. Developers who know one language from several
                        families can see these ideas coming. A practical portfolio: one systems language (Rust or C),
                        one managed workhorse (Java, C#, Go or Kotlin), TypeScript for the web, Python for data and
                        glue, and one functional language (Elixir, F#, Clojure or Haskell). The last one will change
                        how you write the other four.
                    </p>
                    <button type="button" class="ls-show" data-ls-show="compare:rust typescript python">Compare Rust, TypeScript and Python</button>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="matrix" class="ls-section ls-wide" aria-labelledby="matrix-heading">
            <div class="ls-section__intro">
                <p class="ls-kicker">09 · The matrix</p>
                <h2 id="matrix-heading" class="ls-h2">Everything in one table</h2>
                <p class="ls-lede">
                    The reference view. Sort by any column, switch to traits for the technical details, or select a
                    language for its profile.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="ls-toolbar">
                <div class="ls-segmented" role="tablist" aria-label="Matrix view">
                    <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="matrix-scores" id="tab-scores" data-ls-tab="scores">Scores</button>
                    <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="matrix-traits" id="tab-traits" data-ls-tab="traits">Traits</button>
                </div>
                <div class="ls-heat-legend" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span>Score</span>
                    <i data-heat="1">1–2</i><i data-heat="2">3–4</i><i data-heat="3">5–6</i><i data-heat="4">7–8</i><i data-heat="5">9–10</i>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div id="matrix-scores" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-scores" class="ls-scroll" data-ls-panel="scores">
                <table class="ls-table ls-matrix" data-ls-matrix>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button type="button" data-ls-sort="name" data-type="text">Language</button></th>
                            <th scope="col" aria-sort="ascending"><button type="button" data-ls-sort="rank">Popularity</button></th>
